who wants to leave to go to a boarding school or maybe they have a dream of going to California or something like that, that's a choice they're, but not a choice they're required to make in order to have the kind of middle-class life that their parents have and the people around them have. So we all love to hear stories of poor students who are the first in their families to go to college. You know, we applaud first-generation college students for their grit, for their determination. We don't usually talk very much about the social and emotional cost they might be paying. And I'm wondering if those costs might be behind some of the astonishing

